WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.481
Label Review Workbench helps a reviewer compare alcohol label artwork with application information.

00:00:05.481 --> 00:00:08.525
This is the actual application, using fictional labels.

00:00:08.525 --> 00:00:13.452
Start by loading a sample or uploading a new image, then enter what the application says.

00:00:13.452 --> 00:00:16.608
The image is read independently of those expected values.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.147
Select Review label to run real server-side extraction.

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:27.926
A vision model transcribes the artwork, and optical character recognition checks the supporting text.

00:00:27.926 --> 00:00:30.959
The workbench then applies explicit comparison rules.

00:00:30.959 --> 00:00:35.079
Each finding shows the expected value, the observed value, and a reason.

00:00:35.079 --> 00:00:38.512
The processing time shown here is measured for this request.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:47.086
Suppose the application says forty percent alcohol, while the label shows forty-five percent and ninety proof.

00:00:47.086 --> 00:00:49.465
Change the expected value and review again.

00:00:49.465 --> 00:00:51.789
The alcohol finding identifies a mismatch.

00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:59.480
Ordinary brand capitalization and apostrophe differences can still match, while conflicting alcohol and proof values require a closer look.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.704
The government warning receives a stricter check.

00:01:04.704 --> 00:01:09.670
This fictional label changes the required wording, and the workbench flags the difference.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.153
Heading capitalization is checked separately.

00:01:12.153 --> 00:01:16.898
Font weight, physical print size, contrast, and placement still need human inspection.

00:01:16.898 --> 00:01:20.816
A content match never means the label has received regulatory approval.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.183
For a batch, select the label images and a JSON manifest that supplies the expected information for each file.

00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:33.792
The queue supports up to three hundred items, with two requests running at a time.

00:01:33.792 --> 00:01:37.276
Each result is independent, and transient retries are bounded.

00:01:37.276 --> 00:01:40.424
Keep the tab open, then export the summary and findings.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.018
Reviewers can download JSON and CSV results, including reasons and observed text.

00:01:48.018 --> 00:01:54.709
The repository explains the AWS architecture, test coverage, measured evaluation, and remaining limitations.

00:01:54.709 --> 00:01:58.550
Temporary storage and request allowances bound this prototype.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:03.568
AI assists the comparison; a human reviewer remains responsible for the decision.
